       If you completely hide the short forms of the Perl public API, add
       -DPERL_NO_SHORT_NAMES to the compilation flags.  This means that for
       example instead of writing

           warn("%d bottles of beer on the wall", bottlecount);

       you will have to write the explicit full form

           Perl_warn(aTHX_ "%d bottles of beer on the wall", bottlecount);

       (See "Background and MULTIPLICITY" in perlguts for the explanation of
       the "aTHX_". )  Hiding the short forms is very useful for avoiding all
       sorts of nasty (C preprocessor or otherwise) conflicts with other
       software packages (Perl defines about 2400 APIs with these short names,
       take or leave few hundred, so there certainly is room for conflict.)

